From: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 16:15:50 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] [EME] Decode external CDM video into dma-bufs

https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=328385

Reviewed by NOBODY (OOPS!).

webkitexternalcdmvideodec puts the CDM's I420 pictures out in system
memory, so a sink that shows dma-bufs, a display plane or the
compositor's EGL import, needs them uploaded or copied first, which
costs a core at 720p50 on small boards. The decoder now offers DMA_DRM
caps as well. For an 8-bit 4:2:0 stream whose sink names a dma-heap
and its held frames through the webkit.dma-heap context and takes
YUV420 or NV12, it puts the pictures out in buffers from that heap:
before each CDM call it takes buffers from a pool and hands them to
the CdmAdapter as the call's video output, whose Allocate() gives them
to whichever CDM thread asks for picture memory during the call.
The CDM thus decodes straight into the dma-buf, and for NV12 the chroma
planes are interleaved behind the picture.

The CDM keeps pictures across calls and returns them later, so the
decoder registers every picture it hands out and takes the returned
one back by its address. The pool holds the frames the sink holds, or
the larger min-buffers an allocation query reports once the decoder is
linked, plus the pictures the CDM has been seen to keep, plus the one
the CDM call writes into, plus the frames queued ahead of the sink
during 600 ms.
The pictures the CDM keeps are counted after each output as the
smaller of the pictures still registered and the frames still pending,
so that neither a leaked picture nor input taken without output raises
the count; when the count grows, the decoder negotiates a larger pool
before it puts that picture out, so that a copy at output takes its
buffer from the new pool. Before each call the decoder reserves the
buffers the pool counts for the CDM and the call, less the dma-bufs
the CDM holds, because the CDM can allocate two pictures in a call
that follows one without output. Allocate() never waits, because the
CDM call holds the adapter lock audio decryption shares: a picture the
CDM asks for once the reserved buffers are used up, before the first
pool, or after a size change, goes into heap memory and is copied into
a pool buffer at output. The pool's picture size is the size the CDM
asked for the first picture of a new size, and that picture drops the
buffers still reserved from the old pool; a picture the CDM decoded
into a dma-buf smaller than the new size is copied as well. When the
stream or the sink rules dma-bufs out, the decoder stays with system
memory, and under a hole-punch quirk the player then switches to the
GL sink.

The decoder runs the CDM on a thread of its own, which never takes
GstVideoDecoder's stream lock, and an output thread makes every call
that takes it: finishing, dropping and releasing frames, setting the
output state and negotiating pools, in the order the CDM thread queues
them. gst_video_decoder_finish_frame() holds the stream lock while
downstream blocks the push, so the CDM keeps decoding while playsink's
queue is full, up to the frames the pool queues ahead of the sink. The
output thread also interleaves the chroma and unmaps the dma-buf, with
the layout each picture was made in. handle_frame() maps the input and
queues it for the CDM thread without the stream lock, as many frames as
the queued frames cover, since upstream's next chain() waits for the
lock while the output thread pushes; the CDM thread matches pictures
against its own list of the frames it handed to the CDM. A new output
state or a pool for more held pictures is set up on the output thread
after the pictures queued before it, while the CDM thread waits.
Flushes, drains and caps changes wait for both threads to be idle.

The queued frames, ceil(s_slowCdmCallCover / frame period) with
s_slowCdmCallCover 600 ms, cover the longest CDM call: 15 frames at
25 fps, 30 at 50 fps. Widevine's software CDM spends most of a call
decrypting, at 0.4 to 0.9 ms per KB of encrypted data on Cortex-A72
and Cortex-A55 boards, so a keyframe takes several frame periods; the
longest call measured was 578 ms, a 1080p25 keyframe on an RK3568, and
486 ms at 720p50. The frame period comes from the caps' frame rate, or
without one from the duration of the frame being put out. The pool
allocates a buffer only when none is free, so the queue costs only the
buffers downstream actually holds.

The decoder turns off GstVideoDecoder's QoS handling. The CDM decodes
every frame, since later frames refer to it, so dropping a late frame
saves no work and only discards a decoded picture; and the base class
drops every frame due within twice the lateness after a late one, so
that one slow call also costs frames decoded in time. The sink still
drops frames later than its max-lateness.

